// SOS M3 unit 6: EXECUTABLE IS AN AUTHORITY, NOT A DEFAULT (sawos design 9 D-1;
// spec §2.5, §3).
//
// **X STAYED A PROPERTY OF THE MAPPING; WHICH ACCESS BITS A HANDLE MAY REQUEST
// BECAME A RIGHT.** Design 6's decision is untouched — a region still carries no
// R/W/X triple, which is what lets one region be RO here and RW there — and the
// new bit is one level up: `MemoryRight.MapExecute` says whether this HANDLE may
// ask for `MapAccess.Execute` at all.
//
// THE TRANSCRIPT, read as an order:
//
//   SOS execgate: mapped executable                       <- root's FULL handle
//   SOS execgate: minted a sibling without MapExecute
//   SOS execgate: the sibling maps read-write             <- it is a live handle
//   SOS execgate: asking for execute through it
//   SOS: process fault: access denied
//
// **THE THIRD LINE IS WHAT MAKES THE FOURTH MEAN ANYTHING.** A sibling that
// could not map at all would produce the same refusal for the wrong reason, so
// this program maps read-write through the narrowed handle FIRST and only then
// asks for execute. One handle, two asks, one refusal: the difference is the
// bit, and nothing else.
//
// **IT IS CAPABILITY FLOW, NOT IDENTITY**, which is the whole ruling. The kernel
// never asks who is calling. Root maps executable because root's boot pool root
// carries the bit (`memory_rights()` mints it, and has to — attenuation is
// monotonic, so a bit not minted at boot could never appear later); the sibling
// cannot because the mask that made it left the bit out. "Only root maps
// executable" is therefore something a launcher ARRANGES, once, at the mint
// where it hands memory over — not a rule anybody enforces per call.
//
// THE MASK BELOW IS EXACTLY WHAT A LAUNCHER WOULD WRITE for a driver child's
// RAM, applied here to a sibling root KEEPS. That is the only way to observe the
// refusal in one transcript: a rights refusal is a FAULT, so it ends whichever
// process meets it, and a launcher meeting its own child's refusal would be
// reading it out of a §8 status word instead of off the console.
//
// **W|X IS A DIFFERENT CLAIM AND IS `map-wx-refused`'s.** Both refusals landed
// in this unit and they answer different questions — this one is "may you
// produce an executable row at all", that one is "may ONE row be both writable
// and executable" — so they are two cases, because each is a fault and a fault
// ends the process.

import sos.{System, Process, Memory, Mapping, MapAccess, MemoryRight,
            SosStatus}

/// One page of the pool, which is the smallest thing either profile grants.
static CHUNK: UInt = 0x1000

/// Read and execute — a text mapping, and the one thing this unit gated.
static RX: UInt32 = MapAccess.Read | MapAccess.Execute

/// Read and write, which is what any scratch region wants and what the narrowed
/// handle is still perfectly able to install.
static RW: UInt32 = MapAccess.Read | MapAccess.Write

/// **THE POLICY, AND IT IS THE ONE A LAUNCHER WRITES FOR A CHILD'S RAM.** Keep
/// the ticket that lets the region travel (`Transfer`, the bit `give` checks)
/// and the authority to install it (`Map`). `MapExecute` is absent BY SILENCE,
/// which is the keep mask's polarity: what is not named is not kept, so a bit
/// added to `MemoryRight` tomorrow is denied to every child configured today.
/// `Split` is absent too, which is the honest way to say "here is your RAM, you
/// may not subdivide it for anyone".
static CHILD_RAM: UInt32 = (MemoryRight.Transfer | MemoryRight.Map)

static EXIT_OK: UInt = 0
static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let proc = try system.process_self() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: no process handle\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // One boot region: the RAM pool. Root's config is what says so.
    var pool_record = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        print("SOS execgate: no pool region: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let pool = pool_record.take_memory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: pool record is not a region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let page = try pool.split(len: CHUNK) catch {
        print("SOS execgate: split failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // **THE POSITIVE ARM: ROOT MAPS EXECUTABLE, AND THE ROW INSTALLING IS THE
    // PROOF.** Nothing needs to be EXECUTED out of this page — a page of
    // uninitialised pool is not a program — and asserting the jump would be
    // asserting the loader rather than the gate. What is claimed is exactly that
    // a handle carrying `MemoryRight.MapExecute` may ask for
    // `MapAccess.Execute`, which is a row the kernel installs or a fault it
    // raises, and one of those two things happens on the next line.
    let _text = try proc.map(memory: &page, access: RX) catch {
        print("SOS execgate: mapping executable failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: mapped executable\n")

    // **THE NARROWING**, and it is the ordinary universal mint: a SIBLING onto
    // the same region, in root's own table, carrying root's rights INTERSECTED
    // with the mask. Root's original handle is untouched and still maps
    // executable, which is what "per handle, not per region" means and is stated
    // honestly in `MemoryRight`: a second handle that still carries the bit can
    // map the same bytes X, because design 6 accepted no aliasing bookkeeping.
    let narrow = try page.mint(rights: CHILD_RAM) catch {
        print("SOS execgate: mint failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: minted a sibling without MapExecute\n")

    // THE CONTROL. The narrowed handle is a working Memory handle: it maps
    // read-write, over the same page, into the same domain. Without this line
    // the refusal below would be consistent with the sibling being broken in
    // any of a dozen ways.
    let _data = try proc.map(memory: &narrow, access: RW) catch {
        print("SOS execgate: mapping read-write failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: the sibling maps read-write\n")

    // **AND THE REFUSAL, which is a FAULT and therefore the last thing this
    // program does.** An unspent right ends the caller — the same answer every
    // other one gives, and caller-checkable, because rights are a property of
    // the handle it chose to call through and this program is the one that
    // minted it.
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: asking for execute through it\n")
    match proc.map(memory: &narrow, access: RX) {
        case Ok(_) -> {
            let _ = system.debug_print("SOS execgate: UNREACHABLE\n")
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        },
        case Err(e) -> {
            print("SOS execgate: UNREACHABLE status {}\n", e)
            let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        },
    }
    let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_OK)
}
